WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:05.010
At first, this looks like a perfectly normal
map of New York City.

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:08.510
You zoom in and there’s
Broadway, and Bowery.

00:00:08.510 --> 00:00:13.070
But then you realize that the map is...wrong.

00:00:13.070 --> 00:00:16.960
Streets run diagonally and the map adds land
beyond the edge of the island.

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:18.920
That’s because this wasn’t a map.

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:20.990
It was a plan that was rejected.

00:00:20.990 --> 00:00:25.619
And this one — the one with the perfect
grid we know — was basically what we ended

00:00:25.619 --> 00:00:26.619
up with.

00:00:26.619 --> 00:00:32.130
Today Manhattan has a grid so perfect that
the way the sun shines through it is a hashtag

00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:34.970
on Instagram.

00:00:34.970 --> 00:00:42.720
But these grids were not all about beautiful
design, or even making it easier to find your

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:44.910
way around when you come up out of the subway.

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:48.370
The real reason one of these plans made it
and the other didn’t?

00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:58.140
It says something about how — and why — all
cities develop the way they do.

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:03.410
This is a utopia called Philadelphia.

00:01:03.410 --> 00:01:08.479
When William Penn designed Philadelphia in
1681, he wanted to make an ideal city.

00:01:08.479 --> 00:01:12.299
His intentions reflected American ideas and
his Quakerism.

00:01:12.299 --> 00:01:16.490
The lines on his grid weren’t just right
angles, they were morally right angles.

00:01:16.490 --> 00:01:22.479
He wanted to preserve a sense of a country
town using common areas and gardens.

00:01:22.479 --> 00:01:28.350
In 1733 in Savannah, Georgia, the Oglethorpe
Plan was influenced by the Enlightenment,

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:32.200
with an emphasis on balance and limits on
the grid’s growth.

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:37.610
That resulted in a grid too, but with an elegant
design including common squares where all

00:01:37.610 --> 00:01:44.400
could congregate, and
commons limiting the grid plan’s reach.

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:50.049
Pierre L’enfant’s 1791 plan for Washington,
DC, was even more ambitious, and though the

00:01:50.049 --> 00:01:55.689
city deviated from his design, some flourishes
survived, like diagonal avenues sliced across

00:01:55.689 --> 00:02:02.880
the city, circles to vary the grid’s monotony,
and grand squares for each of the then-15

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:03.950
states.

00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:05.900
New York was never so organized.

00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:11.690
This 1767 map shows the chaotic curved streets
and irregularities that marked the nearly

00:02:11.690 --> 00:02:13.950
150 years of European settlement.

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:18.800
What little order that had developed was largely
private and subject to frequent change.

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:22.730
This 1776 map shows a planned grid with a
large square.

00:02:22.730 --> 00:02:26.690
That square belonged to the Delancey estate
(Delaney was a typo).

00:02:26.690 --> 00:02:30.970
Because the prominent New York family supported
the British during the Revolutionary War,

00:02:30.970 --> 00:02:33.960
the city confiscated their land after the
war.

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:37.870
This 1789 map shows what happened to their
planned square.

00:02:37.870 --> 00:02:39.140
It disappeared.

00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:40.820
New York could have been stuck with this chaos.

00:02:40.820 --> 00:02:45.020
But as the 1800s rolled around, it couldn’t
afford to be much longer.

00:02:45.020 --> 00:02:51.150
It’s tough to know New York’s exact population
before 1800, but the trend was clear - massive

00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:55.350
growth, more than doubling between 1770 and
1790.

00:02:55.350 --> 00:02:59.950
Outbreaks of yellow fever that spread up and
down the East Coast heightened the urgency

00:02:59.950 --> 00:03:02.350
to build a cleaner, more orderly city.

00:03:02.350 --> 00:03:08.340
And that is where this failed plan comes in
— in 1797 the city hired architect Joseph

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:12.370
Mangin and surveyor Casimir Goerck to map
New York.

00:03:12.370 --> 00:03:16.200
The plan showed the city as it “should be,”
not as it was.

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:20.090
They widened janky streets and even added
to the waterfront — they proposed something

00:03:20.090 --> 00:03:28.400
graceful, but the city needed something fast
— so their plan was rejected.

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:31.880
In 1807, the state established a new commission
to create a workable plan,

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:33.840
and it was huge.

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:38.450
In this map, this color shows the settled
land, and this color shows the projected areas

00:03:38.450 --> 00:03:39.460
for the grid.

00:03:39.460 --> 00:03:43.740
In their official report, they said they’d
planned for “a greater population than is

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:46.850
collected at any spot on this side of China.”

00:03:46.850 --> 00:03:48.960
The grid made sense to hold it.

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:54.240
They had debated “whether they should confine
themselves to rectilinear and rectangular

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:59.320
streets, or whether they should adopt some
of those supposed improvements by circles,

00:03:59.320 --> 00:04:05.220
ovals, and stars, which certainly embellish
a plan, whatever may be their effect as to

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:06.580
convenience and utility.”

00:04:06.580 --> 00:04:12.000
Basically, did they want L’enfant’s Washington
or a uniform grid?

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:18.249
They decided that, “right-angled houses
are the most cheap to build and the most convenient

00:04:18.249 --> 00:04:19.329
to live in.

00:04:19.329 --> 00:04:22.749
The effect of these plain and simple reflections
was decisive.”

00:04:22.749 --> 00:04:25.210
The grid did that without screwing up existing
property-lines.

00:04:25.210 --> 00:04:28.620
It was predictable for developers.

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:30.030
This was a different type of design.

00:04:30.030 --> 00:04:35.289
The grid seems orderly to us, but this order
was in service of cheapness and efficiency.

00:04:35.289 --> 00:04:38.629
The city needed to build to keep going.

00:04:38.629 --> 00:04:41.539
What could be more New York than that?

00:04:41.539 --> 00:04:44.629
This plan isn’t for a city, but a park.

00:04:44.629 --> 00:04:48.460
Central Park.

00:04:48.460 --> 00:04:53.189
Landscape architect Frederick Olmsted designed
that park and many other public spaces.

00:04:53.189 --> 00:04:58.039
In a laundry list of criticisms of the New
York City grid, he said that, “Still other,

00:04:58.039 --> 00:05:02.960
and perhaps even graver, misfortunes to the
city...could have been avoided by a different

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:05.469
arrangement of its streets.”

00:05:05.469 --> 00:05:07.879
The dream plan would have been more refined,
but this?

00:05:07.879 --> 00:05:10.419
It just wasn’t practical.

00:05:10.419 --> 00:05:12.319
City plans reflect values.

00:05:12.319 --> 00:05:14.020
And then they shape culture.

00:05:14.020 --> 00:05:17.900
In 1811, New York’s values were build, build,
build.

00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:20.720
So they adopted a plan to do it.

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:23.759
Without development, you just have the sun.

00:05:23.759 --> 00:05:27.490
The buildings make it worth looking at.

00:05:27.490 --> 00:05:34.419
Hey that’s it for this episode about grids.

00:05:34.419 --> 00:05:39.319
I’m about to read some comments from the
previous episode of this Almanac: Road trip

00:05:39.319 --> 00:05:40.319
edition.

00:05:40.319 --> 00:05:41.379
It was all about the Vagabonds.

00:05:41.379 --> 00:05:44.919
“Edison was walking around calling Harvey
Firestone dude.”

00:05:44.919 --> 00:05:49.750
Uh yeah, he was, and also one thing I wasn’t
able to mention was that Edison was really

00:05:49.750 --> 00:05:54.430
hard of hearing at this time of his life,
so basically every time you wanted to talk

00:05:54.430 --> 00:05:57.330
to him you had to yell directly in his ear.

00:05:57.330 --> 00:06:02.779
“A 2019 version would probably be in a Tesla
with Elon Musk, Bill Gates, and Warren Buffet

00:06:02.779 --> 00:06:04.309
and Leonardo diCaprio.”

00:06:04.309 --> 00:06:08.520
I will see you next week, and I’m actually
going to be driving in this episode.

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:10.520
So get ready and buckle up.

